  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Consider the equation in the variable $z,$ given by\n\n$$\n-5(p-9)z=10\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$",
  "worked_solution": "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $az=b.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=-5(p-9),\\,b=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n-5(p-9)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5(p-9) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-5(p-9)}{-5} & =\\frac{0}{-5} \\\\ p-9 & =0 \\\\ p & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
